# 1999.LOC onion service.
#
# Installed at /etc/tor/torrc.d/1999loc.conf by install.sh, which also makes
# sure /etc/tor/torrc includes that directory.

# This box is a server. It does not need a SOCKS port and should not offer
# one: an open SOCKS port on a machine with a public IP is somebody else's
# proxy. (Comment it back in only if you want to curl the onion from the box
# itself, and comment it out again afterwards.)
SocksPort 0

HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/1999loc/
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:8080

# Onion services are v3 and have been for years; stated anyway so a future
# default cannot quietly move underneath us.
HiddenServiceVersion 3

# Refuse to start rather than run with a directory Tor thinks is unsafe. The
# secret key in there is the address.
HiddenServiceDirGroupReadable 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: single-hop mode.
#
# A visitor still gets their full three hops of anonymity. What this drops is
# the three hops on the *service* side, which exist to hide where the service
# is running. This site is already published under a domain name pointing at
# Cloudflare, so the location of the mirror is not a secret being kept, and
# turning these on roughly halves the latency.
#
# The cost is real: with these on, the box's IP is discoverable by anyone who
# cares to look. Turn them on only if that was already true, and never on a
# machine whose location is part of what you are protecting. Both lines are
# required together, and Tor will refuse to start unless SocksPort is 0.
#
# HiddenServiceNonAnonymousMode 1
# HiddenServiceSingleHopMode 1
